WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
A useful way to reason about Kubernetes networking is to follow a request.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.440
A client reaches an ingress controller, gateway, load balancer, or reverse proxy.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.340
That routing layer forwards traffic to a Service.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.720
The Service selects ready Pods using labels.

5
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:24.820
Traffic then reaches a container port inside a Pod, where the Node.js process must be listening on the expected interface and port.

6
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.680
A mistake at any one of those steps can look like an application outage even when the code itself is healthy.

7
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.920
Many production bugs are configuration mismatches.

8
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.980
The Service targets the wrong port.

9
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.040
The Pod labels do not match the Service selector.

10
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.940
The application listens only on localhost when it should accept traffic inside the container.

11
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.640
The Ingress routes the wrong hostname or path.

12
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:54.660
TLS termination happens at a proxy, but the application does not trust forwarded headers correctly.

13
00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:03.560
Cookie security, CORS, redirects, and absolute URLs may all depend on the real deployment path, not the local development path.

14
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.840
Exposure should be deliberate.

15
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:14.740
Internal admin endpoints, debug ports, development servers, and metrics endpoints should not be reachable from the public internet by accident.

16
00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.000
Health endpoints should reveal service status without leaking sensitive details.

17
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.140
Metrics should be available to monitoring systems without becoming an information disclosure path.

18
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:34.640
Network policies, service mesh policy, namespaces, and platform controls can restrict traffic paths where available, but they need clear design intent to be effective.

19
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.280
For Node.js teams, networking review should be part of deployment review.

20
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:56.300
Confirm the container port, Service port, selectors, Ingress or gateway rules, TLS assumptions, trusted proxy settings, CORS behavior, cookie settings, and separation of public, internal, health, and metrics endpoints.

21
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.840
Kubernetes provides several layers of routing and service discovery.

22
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.100
The engineering task is to make sure those layers expose exactly the service boundary you intended, and no more.
